Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 17:48:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(lsp): drop inline completion candidates contradicted
 by input

Problem:

`Completor:show()` computes the longest common prefix between the buffer
text at the item's range and the candidate's first line, then discards
that result with `math.max(skip, cursor_col - col + 1)`. A candidate is
therefore still rendered after the text typed since the request has
contradicted it, trimmed to the cursor so that the screen disagrees with
the buffer. With copilot-language-server, buffer `f` and candidate
`func add(a int, b int) int {` render as `fxnc add(a int, b int) int {`
once `x` is typed, and accepting there applies a candidate the user can
see is wrong.

Solution:

Use the longest common prefix for what it already encodes. An item may
replace the text its own range covers, so the candidate is allowed to
differ from the buffer there; only text typed past the end of that range
can contradict it. Where the common prefix stops inside that typed text,
the candidate is out of date, so drop it rather than trim the
contradiction away. Items with an empty or absent range fall under the
same rule, since for them every character up to the cursor was typed
after the request.

Input that still matches is untouched: it keeps being handled in place by
the existing prefix trim, with no removal and re-add, so no additional
redraw is introduced. The request that follows is the same debounced one
as before, so nothing is displayed for the same interval during which the
contradicted candidate used to be displayed.

Cycling keeps working across a dropped candidate by tracking the selected
index on the completor rather than only on the item it produced.

Fixes #36529
---
 runtime/lua/vim/lsp/inline_completion.lua     |  26 +++-
 .../plugin/lsp/inline_completion_spec.lua     | 137 ++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 159 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/inline_completion.lua b/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/inline_completion.lua
index d016f67..c379d4d 100644
--- a/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/inline_completion.lua
+++ b/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/inline_completion.lua
@@ -63,6 +63,7 @@ local namespace = api.nvim_create_namespace('nvim.lsp.inline_completion')
 ---@field active table<integer, vim.lsp.inline_completion.Completor?>
 ---@field timer? uv.uv_timer_t Timer for debouncing automatic requests
 ---@field current? vim.lsp.inline_completion.Item Currently selected item
+---@field _index? integer Index of the last selected item, kept across invalidation
 ---@field client_state table<integer, vim.lsp.inline_completion.ClientState>
 local Completor = {
   name = 'inline_completion',
@@ -168,6 +169,7 @@ end
 ---@param show_index? boolean
 function Completor:select(index, show_index)
   self.current = nil
+  self._index = index
   local client_id, item = self:get_item(index)
   if not client_id or not item then
     self:hide()
@@ -240,6 +242,23 @@ function Completor:show(hint)
     local cursor_row, cursor_col =
       vim.pos.cursor(self.bufnr, api.nvim_win_get_cursor(winid)):to_extmark()
     if row == cursor_row then
+      -- An item may replace the text its own range covers, not just insert before
+      -- it, so the candidate is allowed to differ from the buffer there. Only text
+      -- typed past that range can contradict it, and once it does the candidate is
+      -- out of date: drop it instead of trimming the contradiction away.
+      local typed_col = cursor_col
+      if current.range then
+        local _, _, end_row, end_col = current.range:to_extmark()
+        if end_row == row then
+          typed_col = end_col
+        end
+      end
+      -- Column at which the buffer stops matching the candidate.
+      local diverged = col + skip - 1
+      if diverged >= typed_col and diverged < cursor_col then
+        self.current = nil
+        return
+      end
       skip = math.max(skip, cursor_col - col + 1)
     end
   end
@@ -417,13 +436,12 @@ function M.select(opts)
   local count = opts.count or vim.v.count1
   local wrap = opts.wrap ~= false
 
-  local current = completor.current
-  if not current then
+  local n = completor:count_items()
+  if n == 0 then
     return
   end
 
-  local n = completor:count_items()
-  local index = current._index + count
+  local index = completor._index + count
   if wrap then
     index = (index - 1) % n + 1
   else
